meta {
  type: collection
}

auth {
  mode: bearer
}

auth:bearer {
  token: {{token}}
}

docs {
  # curlme API

  Every request that needs a session inherits this collection level bearer
  auth, so signing in once fills `{{token}}` for the whole collection.

  ## Getting started

  1. Pick the `Local` environment (or `Production`).
  2. Run `Auth > register` once to create the account, or `Auth > signin` if it
     already exists. Both write `token` into the environment.
  3. Everything else is now authenticated.

  `Admin` needs an account whose role is ADMIN. Put the username in
  `ADMIN_USERNAMES` in `apps/api/.env` and run `npm run db:seed`, or have an
  existing admin promote it through `Admin > set user role`.

  ## Environment variables

  | Variable | Purpose |
  | --- | --- |
  | `baseUrl` | API origin |
  | `username`, `password`, `name`, `designation`, `bio` | registration and sign in payload |
  | `token` | bearer token, written by register and signin (secret) |
  | `targetUsername`, `targetPassword` | the second account the follow and admin user routes act on |
  | `postId`, `replyId` | written by create post and create reply |
  | `deviceCode` | written by the GitHub device flow start |

  ## Running the whole collection

  Folders are sequenced so a top to bottom run tells a story: register both
  accounts, sign in, read the catalogue and the profiles, follow and unfollow,
  post, reply, like, then delete.

      bru run -r --env Local --delay 1500 --exclude-tags destructive,manual

  `--delay` is not optional. Every route sits behind a limiter of 10 requests
  per 10 seconds per IP, so an undelayed run 429s from about the seventh
  request onward.

  Two tags exist to keep that run clean:

  - `destructive` marks the requests that would break what follows, notably
    `Posts > delete post`, which removes the very post the `Replies` folder
    needs.
  - `manual` marks the GitHub device flow, which needs a browser step in the
    middle and so cannot run unattended.

  The run is idempotent. Requests that would collide on a second run
  (`register`, `register follow target`, `follow user`, `create designation`)
  accept the conflict status alongside the created one, so nothing has to be
  reset between runs.
}
